  ],
  "slug": "untitled-topic-5b22",
  "summary": "Fixed-topology assumptions and bootstrap confidence limits are now being tested directly against viral and phylogenomic benchmarks.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these items is evaluating the reliability of estimated phylogenetic structure, not only point estimates. A benchmark of fixed-tree-topology strategies on viral datasets finds substitution-model parameters robust, while clock rates and node ages are sensitive to the topology assumption. Net Bootstrap Support offers an efficient bootstrap-upsampling approach that flags phylogenetic signal heterogeneity and overconfidence in phylogenomic inference of organismal relationships. Bayesian model averaging over parametric coalescent growth models addresses model choice in phylodynamic inference. A T-shaped alignment study tests how the proportion of near full-length HIV-1 genomes, mixed with partial pol sequences, affects tree similarity and transmission cluster inference. What is new is treating topology, support, and model choice as explicit targets of validation. These items are not uniformly network-based; the reticulate framing applies most directly to the heterogeneity and overconfidence diagnostics.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-30"
}
